 .El
+.Sh CLIENT WINDOW ACTION
+The MiOS
+.Ic client-window-action
+command performs a guarded window action for an exact terminal client.
+It requires
+.Fl t Ar exact-client-name ,
+.Fl C Ar expected-client-pid ,
+.Fl I Ar client-instance ,
+.Fl s Ar session-id ,
+.Fl w Ar window-id ,
+.Fl p Ar pane-id
+and
+.Fl P Ar expected-pane-pid .
+All flags precede the final action,
+.Ic zoom .
+.Pp
+The invoking peer and target client must have the same UID.
+The target must be an attached, writable, nonsuspended terminal client.
+The session, window and pane must match its actual input focus, including an
+.Li active-pane
+override. Hidden or exited panes are refused.
+The checks and zoom toggle run synchronously in the server event loop:
+there is no default-client lookup or deferred shell callback.
+Success prints exactly
+.Li ok ;
+invalid or stale identities return an error without changing the layout.
+.Pp
+The action uses the same shared-window zoom tree as
+.Ic resize-pane
+.Fl Z .
+Both tiled and floating panes retain their process, PTY and saved layout.
+Clients sharing that window observe the standard shared zoom state; this
+command does not create per-client layouts.
+.Pp
+The dedicated
+.Li client_pane_id , client_pane_pid , client_window_id
+and
+.Li client_window_zoomed_flag
+formats describe the client's actual input pane without changing the meaning
+of existing pane formats.
+They are empty when that live pane is unavailable.
+.Li client_window_action_version
+is
+.Li 1
+when these fields and the guarded action are supported.
+.Sh CLIENT POINTER
+The MiOS
+.Ic client-pointer
+command provides a native per-client mouse projection for a persistent control
+client. It requires
+.Fl t Ar exact-client-name ,
+.Fl P Ar expected-client-pid
+and
+.Fl I Ar client-instance
+(the decimal
+.Li client_instance
+format). The target must be an attached writable terminal client; the invoking
+client must be a control client. A read-only control client is permitted.
+.Pp
+The final argument is
+.Ic begin , show , hide , end , move , down , up
+or
+.Ic wheel .
+.Ic begin
+leases the pointer to that control connection and centers it, initially hidden.
+.Ic move
+requires signed
+.Fl x
+and
+.Fl y
+terminal cell coordinates including status rows; with
+.Fl r
+they are deltas, each from -64 to 64. Absolute values are from -32767 to 32767,
+then clamped to the target terminal viewport.
+.Ic down
+and
+.Ic up
+require
+.Fl b Ar left|middle|right .
+.Ic wheel
+requires signed X/Y detents (positive right/up), each at most 32 in magnitude
+and combined at most 32. All flags precede the final action.
+.Pp
+Successful commands print
+.Li "pointer-v1 INSTANCE X Y VISIBLE BUTTON_MASK"
+inside the standard control response. Bits 0, 1 and 2 of the mask are left,
+middle and right. Errors do not mutate pointer state. One control connection
+owns the lease; other clients cannot update it. The queue is bounded to 64
+injected events with consecutive motion coalescing. Mouse bindings, menus,
+status hit testing and pane drag/resize use the normal native input path.
+.Pp
+.Ic hide , end
+or a lost target/control connection cancels owned drag/click state and
+neutralizes only button presses actually delivered to app PTYs, without firing
+mouse-up title or menu actions. Pending events from the old lease are ignored.
+Physical terminal mouse input takes over immediately; a virtual lease cannot
+begin while physical buttons or a drag are held. The visible pointer uses the
+terminal cursor without changing screen cells or pane layout. Hiding or ending
+it restores the application's cursor.
 .Sh KEY BINDINGS
 .Nm
 allows a command to be bound to most keys, with or without a prefix key.
